IMPORT prelude

-- Optometrists and Opticians Act 2007 (Singapore) -- what counts as opticianry and optometry (s 2, the Schedule), who may do it
-- (ss 3, 25(6), 35(2), 39), unlawful practice and unlawful employment (s 25), fees for unlawful work (s 26), the registered
-- person's deadlines (ss 13(3)-(5), 16(3), 17(2), 18(4)-(6), 22, 24(3)), the Board's disciplinary measures (s 20(3), (4)), the
-- maximum penalties (ss 13(4), 17(3), 18(7), 25(4), 27, 31(4)) and the cap on composition (s 29).
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition ("incorporates all amendments up to and including 1 December 2021"), informal consolidation, version
-- in force from 5/12/2025, as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/OOA2007.txt. The latest amendment annotated is Act 19 of
-- 2025 wef 05/12/2025 (s 34, service of documents); Act 11 of 2023 wef 01/05/2023 and S 249/2009 (the Schedule) are also shown.
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Requirement REQ-0021 in ../../../requirements.jsonl: Tier 2 of the remaining Singapore Acts, ordered by
-- everyday-life relevance. The requirement asks what the Act decides for a person or business it applies to; no scenario has asked a
-- sharper question yet. SCOPED to what an optician, optometrist, eye-care shop or customer meets: which acts are reserved and to
-- whom, the offences and penalties, the deadlines, and discipline. Not encoded: the Board, its membership and procedure (ss 4-11),
-- the Registrar and the contents of the registers (ss 12-14, 19), the regulations (s 15(3), s 37) and the classes of registration,
-- qualifications and fees they set (none was retrieved), refusal grounds (s 16(1)), costs (s 21), committees, inspectors' powers and
-- the assessor (ss 30-32), service of documents (s 34), Ministerial exemption orders (s 35(1), none retrieved), s 40, and holding out
-- (s 25(1)(b)) and the "prescribed conditions" of practice in s 25(1).
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill. No pipeline, no coverage table,
-- no independent test pass, no human gate. See NOTES.md.

§ `Optometrists and Opticians Act 2007 -- practice`

§§ `2 and the Schedule -- opticianry and optometry`

-- s 2: "'opticianry' ... means any of the acts or activities specified in Part 1 of the Schedule"; "'optometry' ... Part 2".
-- "'optical appliances' means lenses, spectacles, eyeglasses, artificial eyes, contact lenses or appurtenances thereto for the aid or
-- correction of visual or ocular anomalies of the eyes".
-- Schedule Part 1 (opticianry): 1. "Refraction for persons of 8 years of age or older." 2. Interpreting prescriptions. 3. "Supplying,
-- preparing and dispensing of all optical appliances, EXCEPT FOR CONTACT LENSES". 4. Fitting, adjusting and adapting them, except
-- contact lenses.
-- Schedule Part 2 (optometry): 1. "Refraction." 2. "Prescribing of optical appliances and contact lenses". 3. Detecting
-- abnormalities by binocular vision tests, ophthalmoscopy or fundoscopy, retinoscopy, slit-lamp examination, tonometry, visual field
-- testing. 4. Interpreting prescriptions. 5, 6. Supplying, dispensing and fitting optical appliances AND contact lenses.
--
-- Opticianry has no prescribing item at all: an optician may measure the power of vision ("refraction", s 2) but not prescribe.
-- "Supplying ... all optical appliances" draws no line at ready-made reading glasses; treating them as optical appliances is this
-- encoding's literal reading, not something the text says in terms.
DECLARE Activity IS ONE OF
    `refraction`
    `prescribing spectacles`
    `prescribing contact lenses`
    `tonometry`
    `interpreting a prescription`
    `dispensing spectacles`
    `selling ready-made reading glasses`
    `fitting contact lenses`

GIVEN `the activity` IS AN Activity, `the client's age` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the activity is opticianry` IF
    CONSIDER `the activity`
    WHEN `refraction` THEN `the client's age` AT LEAST 8
    WHEN `interpreting a prescription` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `dispensing spectacles` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `selling ready-made reading glasses` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- Every item of Part 1 is also in Part 2, without the age limit or the contact-lens exception.
GIVEN `the activity` IS AN Activity
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the activity is optometry` IF TRUE

§§ `3, 25(6), 35(2), 39 -- who may do it`

-- s 25(6): "'qualified person' ... means a person who is (a) registered to carry out; and (b) has in force a practising certificate
-- which authorises the person to carry out, that practice". s 20(8): while suspended, a person "is not regarded as a registered
-- person".
-- s 3: "This Act does not apply to any ophthalmologist in relation to his or her practice of optometry or opticianry." (A medical
-- practitioner who is not an ophthalmologist gets no such carve-out.)
-- s 35(2): nothing prohibits a person "undergoing a course of instruction ... accredited by the Board" from engaging in any aspect of
-- the practice "as a part of the course of instruction".
-- s 39(1): every licensed contact lens practitioner under the repealed Contact Lens Practitioners Act "is deemed to be registered as
-- an OPTICIAN and have in force a practising certificate which authorises [him or her] to carry out any practice of opticianry",
-- subject to conditions the Minister may prescribe. Opticianry, as the Schedule now reads, excludes contact lenses.
DECLARE Practitioner HAS
    `is an ophthalmologist` IS A BOOLEAN
    `is a student on an accredited course, acting as part of it` IS A BOOLEAN
    `registered as an optometrist` IS A BOOLEAN
    `has an optometry practising certificate` IS A BOOLEAN
    `registered as an optician` IS A BOOLEAN
    `has an opticianry practising certificate` IS A BOOLEAN
    `was a licensed contact lens practitioner before 22 February 2008` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the person` IS A Practitioner
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person is qualified for optometry` IF
        `the person`'s `registered as an optometrist`
    AND `the person`'s `has an optometry practising certificate`

GIVEN `the person` IS A Practitioner
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person is qualified for opticianry` IF
        (`the person`'s `registered as an optician` AND `the person`'s `has an opticianry practising certificate`)
    OR  `the person`'s `was a licensed contact lens practitioner before 22 February 2008`

GIVEN `the person` IS A Practitioner, `the activity` IS AN Activity, `the client's age` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person may lawfully do it` IF
        `the person`'s `is an ophthalmologist`
    OR  `the person`'s `is a student on an accredited course, acting as part of it`
    OR  (`the person is qualified for optometry` `the person` AND `the activity is optometry` `the activity`)
    OR  (`the person is qualified for opticianry` `the person` AND `the activity is opticianry` `the activity` `the client's age`)

§§ `25(2), (5) -- employing an unqualified person`

-- s 25(2): "A person must not employ any person who is not a qualified person ... to carry out that practice".
-- (5): a defence "for the accused to prove that the accused (a) did not know that the person employed was not a qualified person
-- ...; AND (b) had exercised due diligence to ascertain if the person was a qualified person". Both limbs are needed.
GIVEN `employs an unqualified person to do it` IS A BOOLEAN, `proves lack of knowledge` IS A BOOLEAN, `proves due diligence` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the employer is guilty under section 25(2)` IF
        `employs an unqualified person to do it`
    AND NOT (`proves lack of knowledge` AND `proves due diligence`)

§§ `26 -- no fee for unlawful work`

-- s 26: "A person is not entitled to demand, claim, accept, receive, retain or sue for or recover any fee or charge in any court for
-- any act done in contravention of section 25." Even a fee already paid may not be RETAINED.
GIVEN `the act contravened section 25` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person may keep the fee` IF NOT `the act contravened section 25`

§§ `13(3)-(5) -- telling the Registrar of a change`

-- s 13(3)(a): a registered person must inform the Registrar in writing of any change in name, residential address, practice address
-- or other prescribed particulars "within 28 days of the occurrence of the change". (4): fine up to $1,000.
-- (5): a person who reports a change of residential address under s 10 of the National Registration Act 1965 "is deemed to have
-- complied with subsection (3)(a) in relation to the person's residential address on the date on which the person makes the report".
-- The deeming covers the residential address only; an NRA report does not tell the Registrar of a new practice address.
GIVEN `days after the change` IS A NUMBER, `the change is of residential address` IS A BOOLEAN, `told only by an NRA report` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person has complied with section 13(3)(a)` IF
        `days after the change` AT MOST 28
    AND ((NOT `told only by an NRA report`) OR `the change is of residential address`)

§§ `16(3), 22 -- appeals`

-- s 16(3): a person aggrieved by a refusal of registration may, "within 30 days of the notice", appeal to the Minister "whose decision
-- is final".
-- s 22(1): a person aggrieved by a disciplinary decision under s 20(3), (4) or (5) may, "within 14 days of the date of the decision
-- ... or within such further period as the General Division of the High Court may allow", appeal to the General Division; (2) no
-- further appeal.
GIVEN `days after the notice` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the appeal against refusal is in time` IF `days after the notice` AT MOST 30

GIVEN `days after the decision` IS A NUMBER, `the High Court allowed a further period covering it` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the disciplinary appeal is in time` IF
        `days after the decision` AT MOST 14
    OR  `the High Court allowed a further period covering it`

§§ `17(2), 18(4)-(6) -- certificates`

-- s 18(4): renewal of a practising certificate "must be made no later than 30 days before the expiry"; (5): one who applies "later
-- than 30 days before the expiry ... is liable to pay ... such late application fee as may be prescribed" (amount not retrieved).
-- ss 17(2), 18(6): after cancellation or suspension under s 20, the certificate of registration and the practising certificate must
-- be surrendered "(a) within 14 days of being notified" or "(b) in a case where an appeal is pending under section 22, within such
-- period as the Board may specify". Fine up to $5,000 for each (ss 17(3), 18(7)).
GIVEN `days before expiry` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a late application fee is payable` IF `days before expiry` LESS THAN 30

GIVEN `an appeal is pending` IS A BOOLEAN, `the period the Board specified` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the days allowed to surrender` MEANS
    IF `an appeal is pending` THEN `the period the Board specified` ELSE 14

§§ `20(3), (4) -- discipline`

-- s 20(3): the Board may cancel registration if the person (a) obtained it "by a fraudulent or an incorrect statement"; (b) had the
-- qualification withdrawn; (c) had registration in another country withdrawn, suspended or cancelled; (d) "has ceased to carry on the
-- practice"; (e) breached a condition of registration; (f) contravened a practice regulation; (g) was convicted of fraud or
-- dishonesty; (h) was convicted of an offence implying a defect in character; (i) "professional misconduct or negligence"; (j) improper
-- conduct rendering him or her unfit; (k) is unable to practise safely by reason of disability; (l) failed to pay a s 20(4)(b) penalty.
-- s 20(4): "Where a registered person is liable to have his or her registration cancelled on any ground mentioned in subsection (3)(e)
-- to (l), the Board may, INSTEAD of cancelling", (a) censure; (b) "a penalty not exceeding $10,000"; (c) conditions "for a period not
-- exceeding 3 years"; (d) suspension "for a period not exceeding 3 years".
-- So on grounds (a) to (d) the Act offers cancellation and nothing short of it (s 20(5) dismissal of a complaint without merit apart).
DECLARE Ground IS ONE OF
    `registration obtained by an incorrect statement`
    `qualification withdrawn`
    `foreign registration cancelled`
    `ceased to practise`
    `breach of a registration condition`
    `conviction for dishonesty`
    `professional negligence`
    `unpaid disciplinary penalty`

GIVEN `the ground` IS A Ground
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Board may impose a lesser measure instead of cancelling` IF
    CONSIDER `the ground`
    WHEN `registration obtained by an incorrect statement` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `qualification withdrawn` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `foreign registration cancelled` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `ceased to practise` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

DECLARE Measure HAS
    `penalty` IS A NUMBER
    `months of conditions` IS A NUMBER
    `months of suspension` IS A NUMBER

GIVEN `the measure` IS A Measure
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the measure is within section 20(4)` IF
        `the measure`'s `penalty` AT MOST 10000
    AND `the measure`'s `months of conditions` AT MOST 36
    AND `the measure`'s `months of suspension` AT MOST 36

§§ `24(3) -- restoration`

-- s 24(3): "No application for re-registration is to be made to the Board (a) before the expiry of 3 years from the date of the
-- cancellation; and (b) more than once in any period of 12 months."
-- Months are counted whole; the encoding treats month 36 as the expiry of 3 years and month 12 as a fresh 12-month period.
GIVEN `months since cancellation` IS A NUMBER, `has applied before` IS A BOOLEAN, `months since the last application` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a re-registration application may be made` IF
        `months since cancellation` AT LEAST 36
    AND ((NOT `has applied before`) OR `months since the last application` AT LEAST 12)

§§ `13(4), 17(3), 18(7), 25(4), 27, 31(4) -- penalties`

-- s 13(4): $1,000. ss 17(3), 18(7): $5,000. s 25(4): "a fine not exceeding $25,000 or ... imprisonment for a term not exceeding
-- 6 months or ... both and, in the case of a second or subsequent conviction, ... $50,000 or ... 12 months". s 27 (fraudulent
-- registration, false entries, forged, bought or sold certificates): $10,000 or 2 years. s 31(4) (refusing or obstructing an
-- inspector): $5,000 or 6 months.
DECLARE Offence IS ONE OF
    `failing to notify a change of particulars`
    `failing to surrender a certificate of registration`
    `failing to surrender a practising certificate`
    `unlawful practice or employment, first conviction`
    `unlawful practice or employment, repeat conviction`
    `fraudulent registration or a forged certificate`
    `obstructing an inspector`

GIVEN `the offence` IS AN Offence
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum fine` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the offence`
    WHEN `failing to notify a change of particulars` THEN 1000
    WHEN `failing to surrender a certificate of registration` THEN 5000
    WHEN `failing to surrender a practising certificate` THEN 5000
    WHEN `unlawful practice or employment, first conviction` THEN 25000
    WHEN `unlawful practice or employment, repeat conviction` THEN 50000
    WHEN `fraudulent registration or a forged certificate` THEN 10000
    WHEN `obstructing an inspector` THEN 5000

GIVEN `the offence` IS AN Offence
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum imprisonment in months` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the offence`
    WHEN `unlawful practice or employment, first conviction` THEN 6
    WHEN `unlawful practice or employment, repeat conviction` THEN 12
    WHEN `fraudulent registration or a forged certificate` THEN 24
    WHEN `obstructing an inspector` THEN 6
    OTHERWISE 0

§§ `29 -- composition`

-- s 29(1): the Board may compound an offence "that is prescribed as a compoundable offence" by collecting "a sum not exceeding the
-- lower of the following: (a) one half of the amount of the maximum fine that is prescribed for the offence; (b) $2,000."
-- Which offences are prescribed as compoundable was not retrieved; the cap is computed for any offence.
GIVEN `the first sum` IS A NUMBER, `the second sum` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the lower of` MEANS IF `the first sum` AT MOST `the second sum` THEN `the first sum` ELSE `the second sum`

GIVEN `the offence` IS AN Offence
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum composition sum` MEANS
    `the lower of` (`the maximum fine` `the offence` DIVIDED BY 2) 2000
